  ],
  "headline": "Intake mapped the requested delivery and identified an existing filter defect and an unverified reporting window.",
  "outcome": "done",
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "K1.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Agents and API clients use ticket transition and list HTTP endpoints; requesters receive notifications through the existing notifier and mail relay; operators inspect the outbox and audit; support leads consume the weekly report."
    },
    {
      "id": "K1.2",
      "basis": "S7",
      "answer": "Green meaningful tests and observed correct behavior against a running server, including close notification, suppression, batching, combined filters, audit and a 500-ticket bulk close."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Scope is this help desk service: closure notifications and batching, the closed-ticket window, combined filters, bulk closure, audit and relevant tests and documentation. Outbox retry changes and migration from OldTracker are outside scope."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Preserve the lifecycle, final closed state, existing error envelope and single-ticket API behavior, creation and resolution notifications, outbox retry and at-least-once delivery, persistence, worker-off behavior, and the CLI raw-column positions used by the weekly report."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Existing interfaces include POST /tickets/:id/transition with {\"to\":\"closed\"}, GET /tickets with status, priority, limit and cursor, GET /tickets/:id/audit, GET /admin/outbox, and npm test. The repository has no bulk-close route or date-window parameter. Provisional default: POST /tickets/bulk/transition with {\"ids\":[\"T-0001\"],\"to\":\"closed\"} and a closed-since list filter; exact names and response shape remain subject to discovery and compatibility review."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Existing transition succeeds with 200 and a ticket; invalid inputs use 400 invalid, missing tickets or routes 404 not_found, disallowed transitions 409 conflict, and faults 500 internal, all errors in {\"error\":{\"code\":\"...\",\"message\":\"...\"}}. Provisional bulk default: reject invalid input with 400, missing tickets with 404, and disallowed or already-closed tickets with 409 before changing any ticket; return 200 with results on success. Discovery must check client expectations before fixing this new contract."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Keep the existing lifecycle and require 500 eligible tickets in one bulk call. Provisional notification policy: suppress a close notice for the same ticket when its resolution notice is pending or was sent within one hour; group close notices by requester within a bulk call and enqueue them immediately for the next worker tick. These thresholds and pending-notice treatment are assumptions, not established policy. The reporting window remains unresolved."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Existing invalid inputs include malformed or non-object JSON, unknown body fields, invalid statuses or priorities, limit outside 1–100, malformed cursors, oversized bodies and X-Actor over 64 characters. Provisional bulk validation rejects empty or duplicate IDs, malformed IDs and more than 500 IDs; the new window parameter must reject malformed or out-of-range values once its contract is settled."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The service owns tickets, append-only audit entries and outbox events in one process; DATA_FILE persists them across restarts, otherwise they last only for the process. NOTIFY_FILE holds delivered JSON lines for the relay, or deliveries stay in memory. A durable batching or suppression design must account for restart behavior."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The API is described as internal-network-only; no authentication or per-user authorization is documented. X-Actor is caller-supplied and defaults to system, so it is not verified identity. The data-file decision assigns one process ownership; concurrent multi-process writers are not supported. With DATA_FILE, saved state reloads after restart; delivery remains at least once. Default: introduce no new visibility or authentication model."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "CommonJS JavaScript on Node.js 22 or later."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.2",
      "basis": "S11",
      "answer": "Use Node's standard library only; add no dependencies."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.3",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Existing settings are PORT=4180, optional DATA_FILE and NOTIFY_FILE, WORKER_INTERVAL_MS=500 with 0 disabling the worker, MAX_BODY_BYTES=65536, and LOG_REQUESTS off by default. Default: require no new configuration; verify that a 500-ID request fits the body limit."
    },
    {
      "id": "K7.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The existing seams are the HTTP handler and validation, store, lifecycle and query functions, clock, persistence, outbox, worker, notifier and templates. Keep notification decisions and batching testable without changing retry behavior."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Node's built-in node:test and node:assert; npm test runs node --test."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.2",
      "basis": "S7",
      "answer": "Use behavioral tests at store, query and notification boundaries plus a running-server check through HTTP, audit and outbox and observable notifier delivery. Exercise suppression boundaries, recipient grouping, combined filters and pagination, repeated closure, restart behavior and 500-ticket bulk closure."
    },
    {
      "id": "K9.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Deliver the change to this repository for the existing Node service; the eventual run needs a writable isolated checkout and permission to run tests and a local server. This intake has read-only access and starts no server. The source checkout remains at its snapshot until the final return; a deployed service changes only after a separate release."
    },
    {
      "id": "K10.1",
      "basis": "S13",
      "answer": "A single bulk call must handle 500 closures; responsiveness is required but no numeric latency target was supplied. Preserve existing internal-network and data-handling assumptions and keep documentation accurate. No new accessibility or UI requirements were stated."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Likely rejection risks are using the wrong reporting window, sending duplicate or missing notices, treating immediate delivery and batching incompatibly, altering retry behavior, breaking clients or audit, and failing the 500-ticket call."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Early evidence is the weekly-report source and its owner’s reporting definition, a combined-filter boundary test, notification timing and failure cases, and a 500-ticket request against a running server."
    },
    {
      "id": "K12.1",
      "basis": "S7",
      "answer": "Terms requiring interpretation are until loop, back-chain, ralph-style, OldTracker, the usual weekly-report window, just got one, right away, batch, idempotent, and bulk call."
    },
    {
      "id": "K12.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The supplied installed-skills list defines until-loop, backchain and ralph-loop at a high level. Repository documentation defines the API, audit, outbox and current report. OldTracker and the intended reporting window are not defined in the inspected repository; no external search was authorized in this read-only exercise."
    },
    {
      "id": "K12.3",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The usual window points to support leads' weekly reporting practice, but scripts/weekly-report.js counts all listed tickets and specifies no date window. Resolution-notice wording and behavior are in src/templates.js and the outbox documentation. Discovery should seek reporting-owner documentation or an authoritative prior report definition."
    },
    {
      "id": "K12.4",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Low-confidence defaults: one hour means recently notified; batching means one message per requester per bulk call without an added waiting window; immediate means enqueue during the change and deliver on the next enabled worker tick. No reporting-window default is treated as authoritative: a rolling seven days and a previous calendar week lead to different results."
    },
    {
      "id": "K13.1",
      "basis": "S12",
      "answer": "Immediate sending and batching require a timing tradeoff; repeated close can be idempotent in state and side effects while returning 409. A 409 response need not repeat the first response to preserve idempotent effects."
    },
    {
      "id": "K13.2",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "The claim that filters already work together is contradicted by matchesFilters using some although docs/api.md specifies all. The weekly report has no date filter despite the suggested usual window. The service has no bulk endpoint; its resolution template's seven-day reopen text does not establish a reporting window."
    },
    {
      "id": "K13.3",
      "basis": "person",
      "answer": "Read repeated close as no additional mutation, audit entry or notification plus 409; read immediate as the next worker tick and batch within a bulk call. Discovery should first seek an authoritative weekly-window definition; if none exists, a person must choose between a rolling seven-day window and a previous calendar week before the window contract is finalized. Provisional fallback for discussion only: rolling seven days in UTC by closure time, with low confidence."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "S1 [fact]: The outcome belongs in this folder's help desk ticket service. S2 [outcome; assumption]: Notify the requester on closure using the existing notification channel, with a provisional one-hour same-ticket suppression policy for pending or recently sent resolution notices; discovery must establish existing notification policy first, and the recorded default governs implementation if none exists. S3 [outcome; assumption]: Enqueue promptly and group notices by requester within each bulk operation; discovery must establish relay and batching constraints first, with the recorded next-worker-tick default blocking no other work once checked. S4 [open question]: Add a closed-ticket list window; discovery must first seek an authoritative reporting definition, and if unavailable a person must choose its interval, time zone and boundary before the filter contract and acceptance checks are finalized; a rolling seven-day UTC closure-time window is only a low-confidence discussion default. S5 [outcome; fact]: Apply all documented filters together. S6 [fact]: Inspection found an OR implementation where the docs require AND, so verification and correction are needed. S7 [boundary]: Back-chain before implementation and pursue an until-loop exit of green tests plus observed running-server behavior; this intake performs neither planning nor execution. S8 [boundary; default]: Overnight ralph-style operation is optional, not required; if used, record a finite iteration and elapsed-time cap before starting, provisionally 10 iterations or 8 hours, whichever comes first. S9 [outcome]: Use behavioral evidence and accurate, concise documentation. S10 [boundary]: Leave outbox retry logic untouched; the OldTracker migration is context, not a separate deliverable. S11 [boundary]: Add no dependencies. S12 [outcome; interpretation]: Repeated close has no repeated side effects and returns 409; successful closure records the caller-supplied X-Actor, or the existing system default. S13 [outcome]: One bulk call must close 500 eligible tickets; no numeric latency target is supplied. Discovery hints [leads, not verified facts]: support leads and the operations owner, their weekly-report schedule and dashboard, repository report script and API/operations documents, any authorized team workspace or internal reporting documentation, and relay or service owners for notification policy; no channel, private repository or internal site was identified. Interactive state [facts and default]: agents and requesters are identified by existing X-Actor and requester fields, without documented authentication; ticket, audit and outbox state is shared through the service and persists only when DATA_FILE is configured; no separate session or device state is specified; the API is documented as internal-network-only, with no per-user hiding policy, and the default adds none. Visibility [fact]: the result becomes usable in the repository checkout on final return and in a deployed target only after release; this intake changes no files and starts no server."
}
